{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions","id":1186420,"title":"Favourite Satiety Conditions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Beast: The Primordial"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-02-15T14:40:09-08:00","id":1186420,"title":"Favourite Satiety Conditions","content":"the absolute high's and low's are blatantly debilitating...<br /> <br /> if your almost starving your horror wildly lashes out at the world.<br /> <br /> If your Horror's in slumbber you lose all your power's though can take a lul from feeding (Must be handy when tqaking a sabbatical from feeding because of a dangerous Hero)<br /> <br /> <br /> The less extreme versions arent quite as debilitating... each giving a boon and bane.<br /> <br /> Sated is middle ground is dangerous if you fear Heroe's placing Anathema on you, but otherwise it's good with no special bane or boon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-15T16:01:39-08:00","id":1186455,"title":"","content":"At the moment, I've favoring Sated... sorta. My character likes to hover around Satiety 4, so the Low Satiety things are going full power all the time, but he can turn all those on with one expenditure. Though he's also a very Avatism focused character, so he rarely has the need to worry about boosting his Nightmares.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T08:11:18-08:00","id":1186668,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1186455#post1186455\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the moment, I've favoring Sated... sorta. My character likes to hover around Satiety 4, so the Low Satiety things are going full power all the time, but he can turn all those on with one expenditure. Though he's also a very Avatism focused character, so he rarely has the need to worry about boosting his Nightmares.</div> </div> </div> </div>I share more or less the same preference, hovering on the border between Sated and Starving so that the character can have some buffer against the crippling penalties of Ravenous, yet they can activate Low Satiety Atavisms and become immune to Anathemas with one Satiety expenditure at most. I typically prefer Atavism-focused characters, so I give relatively little concern to increased proficiency in Nightmares. Moreover, Slumbering seems an exceedingly dangerous condition to be in, even more so than Ravenous, so I prefer to err on being on the far side of it. Taking a respite from feeding does not really seem to be worth the risks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 02-16-2018, 06:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T08:26:42-08:00","id":1186671,"title":"","content":"I'm a Sated/Starving border type as well. Some of the same reasons as Irioth, but also I prefer to play relatively ethical Beasts, and low Satiety makes it easier to feed ethically. (Slumbering is of course the easiest state to be in from that latter perspective, but you need a seriously heinous bit of feeding to get there).<br /> <br /> I also the like the atmosphere of the Horror being restless and eager, reaching out from me into the surrounding dreamscape.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T10:34:00-08:00","id":1186718,"title":"","content":"I'm inclined toward Gorged, mostly because I like the subtle slow-and-steady approach that the Kinship-oriented end of the scale incentivizes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T11:55:40-08:00","id":1186758,"title":"","content":"Depends on the character, honestly. Some chars I play heavy on Nightmares, others tending towards Atavisms. For the latter, my preference is 4, for the stated reasons of stability and close to safety. For the former, it's 8 or 9.<br /> <br /> <br /> However, regarding Slumbering: Beasts with Looming Presence and/or Relentless Hunter are quite capable to deal with that situation more easily, as getting close to death wakes the Horror.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-16T16:17:15-08:00","id":1186862,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1186671#post1186671\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a Sated/Starving border type as well. Some of the same reasons as Irioth, but also I prefer to play relatively ethical Beasts, and low Satiety makes it easier to feed ethically. (Slumbering is of course the easiest state to be in from that latter perspective, but you need a seriously heinous bit of feeding to get there).</div> </div> </div> </div>Conventional morality concerns aside, Low Satiety feeding often appears to be preferable even from a practical/rational selfishness/utilitarian PoV, for the Beast character and the world alike. Given the huge personal risks, purposefully going into Slumbering out of morality concerns seems to be require so much altruistic dedication from a Beast it is questionable how the character was able to reconcile oneself with their Devouring in the first place (in vampire terms, these would be the characters that may be expected to walk in the sun soon after the Embrace). Last but not least, dying while Slumbering may leave the Horror free to rampage indefinitely across the Astral as an Unfettered, and quite possibly do much more mischief than a cautious Beast.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 02-16-2018, 07:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-17T01:28:00-08:00","id":1186983,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1186862#post1186862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Conventional morality concerns aside, Low Satiety feeding often appears to be preferable even from a practical/rational selfishness/utilitarian PoV, for the Beast character and the world alike. Given the huge personal risks, purposefully going into Slumbering out of morality concerns seems to be require so much altruistic dedication from a Beast it is questionable how the character was able to reconcile oneself with their Devouring in the first place (in vampire terms, these would be the characters that may be expected to walk in the sun soon after the Embrace). Last but not least, dying while Slumbering may leave the Horror free to rampage indefinitely across the Astral as an Unfettered, and quite possibly do much more mischief than a cautious Beast.</div> </div> </div> </div>Going into Slumbering has a few benefits, though, e.g. throwing a Hero off of your trail or confuse certain enemies (e.g. Demons could be confused at the thing they always felt was a Beast suddenly turn into a normal human being). Going into deep cover, basically. In a way, it's the opposite of Demon's Going Loud, you're going Very Quiet.<br /> It's risky business, yes, but it has it's benefits.<br /> Or maybe your Character realized that opportunities to feed become rarer (or, for the ethical Beast, that feeding any more in this area might have unwanted effects and it's time to move on), and wants to move to a different place. Not having to feed can be beneficial (especially in Dark Eras where travelling was more time-consuming and opportunities to feed while traveling scarce). Maybe your char just wants a respite from the nagging Hunger for a while (Wedding and Honeymoon come to mind as reasons for such a drastic act). Not wanting to risk to drop to Ravenous, so you don't accidently attack your newborn child in their dreams. Plenty of IC reasons that make it an interesting option, imo. Not ideal, but preferable for a given amount of time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-18T12:49:38-08:00","id":1187336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wormwood</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1186983#post1186983\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Going into Slumbering has a few benefits, though, e.g. throwing a Hero off of your trail or confuse certain enemies (e.g. Demons could be confused at the thing they always felt was a Beast suddenly turn into a normal human being). Going into deep cover, basically. In a way, it's the opposite of Demon's Going Loud, you're going Very Quiet.<br /> It's risky business, yes, but it has it's benefits.<br /> Or maybe your Character realized that opportunities to feed become rarer (or, for the ethical Beast, that feeding any more in this area might have unwanted effects and it's time to move on), and wants to move to a different place. Not having to feed can be beneficial (especially in Dark Eras where travelling was more time-consuming and opportunities to feed while traveling scarce). Maybe your char just wants a respite from the nagging Hunger for a while (Wedding and Honeymoon come to mind as reasons for such a drastic act). Not wanting to risk to drop to Ravenous, so you don't accidently attack your newborn child in their dreams. Plenty of IC reasons that make it an interesting option, imo. Not ideal, but preferable for a given amount of time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, I can see the point in most of the arguments you make, and doing no hunt activities surely can make a character less noticeable to Heroes, but as far as I remember, the Slumbering condition does not make a Beast immune to Hero tracking powers or mundane investigation abilities. As far as I can tell, Slumber makes one more, not less, vulnerable to Hero attacks and invasion of one's Lair. If protection from Heroes is the current main concern, Starving, or hovering very close to it, seems the way to go. The character gets combat and stealth Atavisms running at maximum effectiveness, is immune to Anathemas, their Horror is awake, and they are able to enter the Lair safely and defend it effectively.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-19T08:37:00-08:00","id":1187563,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1187336#post1187336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmm, I can see the point in most of the arguments you make, and doing no hunt activities surely can make a character less noticeable to Heroes, but as far as I remember, the Slumbering condition does not make a Beast immune to Hero tracking powers or mundane investigation abilities. As far as I can tell, Slumber makes one more, not less, vulnerable to Hero attacks and invasion of one's Lair. If protection from Heroes is the current main concern, Starving, or hovering very close to it, seems the way to go. The character gets combat and stealth Atavisms running at maximum effectiveness, is immune to Anathemas, their Horror is awake, and they are able to enter the Lair safely and defend it effectively.</div> </div> </div> </div> <br /> I agree that this tactic does nothing to save a Beast from mundane means of investigation; however, tracking powers and Gifts should no longer work against the Beast, since the Beast is functionally human. According to the Corebook <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>p. 321</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">She loses access to all her Atavisms, Nightmares, Beast-specific Merits, and Birthrights. She does not lose access to Kinship abilities, but cannot spend Satiety to power them. She does not gain an Integrity trait <b>but is otherwise human</b>. She cannot spend Satiety for any reason and does not naturally lose Satiety, no matter how many dots of Lair she has.</div> </div> </div> </div> emphasis mine.<br /> So while a Beast loses basically all their powers, neither does the Hero get any of theirs to deal with them. Right? Couldn't find anything on the topic by skipping the book.<br /> <br /> <br /> I might have misunderstood the part about Hero attacks, but still thought I'd keep this here: <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> p.208</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> A Beast whose is under the Ravenous, Starving, Gorged, or Slumbering Conditions cannot be targeted with Anathema.</div> </div> </div> </div> Getting hit with an Anathema is only a problem while Sated.<br /> <br /> <br /> I agree that for physical confrontation, going Starving is the best condition due to the numerous benefits, but there are other ways of fighting off a Hero - use Nightmares, guile and social skills to have them chased off/attacked. Might even fight when Gorged: Satiety of 9 is amazing with Behold My True Form. Most fit-for-fighting Nightmares have strong effects that are extremely valuable (e.g. All your Teeth are Falling Out -&gt; debuffing your enemy to 1-2 successes at most, with the option to reflexively make them fail a roll and a possible penalty to defense). Although I am not sure whether Heroes were subject to Nightmares or immune, which would render my point moot of course.<br /> <br /> Another point in favour of going Slumbering for a time: getting rid of Anathema: while Slumbering, Anathema do not apply to the Beast.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T11:38:56-08:00","id":1188246,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wormwood</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1187563#post1187563\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree that this tactic does nothing to save a Beast from mundane means of investigation; however, tracking powers and Gifts should no longer work against the Beast, since the Beast is functionally human. According to the Corebook emphasis mine.<br /> So while a Beast loses basically all their powers, neither does the Hero get any of theirs to deal with them. Right? Couldn't find anything on the topic by skipping the book.</div> </div> </div> </div>I did not find any specific rules statement on the issue either, but my resoning would be that all Hero Tracking and Gift abilities that are explictly based on having a Beast as a Target, or the Beast character having recently used their supernatural powers, are denied by the Slumbering Condition. On the other hand, if Hero Gifts just enhance the Hero's own skills, or target something else, such as the Beast's Lair, they remain just as effective. So many Hero powers are indeed denied, but if the Hero tracked down the slumbering Beast by mundane means, or using their tracking powers before the Beast got Slumbering, the Beast character is just as vulnerable at Gifts that just enhance the Hero, or allow them to invade the Lair, and the Beast can't use any of their powers for defence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I might have misunderstood the part about Hero attacks, but still thought I'd keep this here: Getting hit with an Anathema is only a problem while Sated. </div> </div> </div>Exacltly. About this, both Ravenous and Slumbering are useful to remove certain Anathemas. IMO, Ravenous is slightly better than Slumbering for this since it is somewhat less burdensome to bear and resolve, and IIRC R-resolved Anathemas are a little more numerous and a little less troublesome than S-resolved ones, but it is such a slight difference of degree that YMMV may easily vary.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I agree that for physical confrontation, going Starving is the best condition due to the numerous benefits, but there are other ways of fighting off a Hero - use Nightmares, guile and social skills to have them chased off/attacked. Might even fight when Gorged: Satiety of 9 is amazing with Behold My True Form. Most fit-for-fighting Nightmares have strong effects that are extremely valuable (e.g. All your Teeth are Falling Out -&gt; debuffing your enemy to 1-2 successes at most, with the option to reflexively make them fail a roll and a possible penalty to defense). Although I am not sure whether Heroes were subject to Nightmares or immune, which would render my point moot of course. </div> </div> </div>Well, no doubt it is fairly easy to build a combat-focused character based on Nightmares specialization that is more or less as effective as one based on Atavisms and Merits. Personally I find the latter rather more congenial for various gameplay reasons (easier Satiety maintainance, greater immediacy and all-around usefulness of Atavisms enhancements, the Atavisms-focused build just looking more like a transhumanist 'supersoldier', and so on) but I acknowledge the validity of the alternative build.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Another point in favour of going Slumbering for a time: getting rid of Anathema: while Slumbering, Anathema do not apply to the Beast. </div> </div> </div>Admittedly, although Ravenous is overall a little more effective than Slumbering to remove Anathemas for good than just suspending them.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T12:14:30-08:00","id":1188254,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1188246#post1188246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I did not find any specific rules statement on the issue either, but my resoning would be that all Hero Tracking and Gift abilities that are explictly based on having a Beast as a Target, or the Beast character having recently used their supernatural powers, are denied by the Slumbering Condition. On the other hand, if Hero Gifts just enhance the Hero's own skills, or target something else, such as the Beast's Lair, they remain just as effective. So many Hero powers are indeed denied, but if the Hero tracked down the slumbering Beast by mundane means, or using their tracking powers before the Beast got Slumbering, the Beast character is just as vulnerable at Gifts that just enhance the Hero, or allow them to invade the Lair, and the Beast can't use any of their powers for defence.</div> </div> </div> </div>100% agreed!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1188246#post1188246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exacltly. About this, both Ravenous and Slumbering are useful to remove certain Anathemas. IMO, Ravenous is slightly better than Slumbering for this since it is somewhat less burdensome to bear and resolve, and IIRC R-resolved Anathemas are a little more numerous and a little less troublesome than S-resolved ones, but it is such a slight difference of degree that YMMV may easily vary.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mostly totally with you.<br /> Only way I see resolving Slumbering easily is with Looming Presence or Relentless Hunter: upon being full with Lethal, the Horror awakens, upon which either there are now unchecked health boxes (+Lair due to Looming Presence being in effect once more) or you can spend 1 Satiety to erease all Lethal and Bashing damage, making this a fairly easy method to return from Slumbering.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1188246#post1188246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, no doubt it is fairly easy to build a combat-focused character based on Nightmares specialization that is more or less as effective as one based on Atavisms and Merits. Personally I find the latter rather more congenial for various gameplay reasons (easier Satiety maintainance, greater immediacy and all-around usefulness of Atavisms enhancements, the Atavisms-focused build just looking more like a transhumanist 'supersoldier', and so on) but I acknowledge the validity of the alternative build.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Admittedly, although Ravenous is overall a little more effective than Slumbering to remove Anathemas for good than just suspending them.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough =)<br /> I also tend towards Atavism-heavy chars (just the cooler powerset) which is why I purposely made a Character that will focus on Nightmares. Therapist treating patients, working with the police, who's a Tempter or Enabler trying to make people give in to their urges (yes, I've been watching Hannibal, why do you ask?), but tries to mitigate the damage with his therapeutic training (it gets worse before it gets better; accepting the truth helps moving on etc.) Nightmares like You Deserve This just seemed like a perfect fit. Counter-intuitively to my normal way of playing, I try to have him in the High Satiety Area, (usually running &quot;lean and mean&quot; chars) and avoided Relentless Hunter and Looming Presence on purpose. Just to try out new stuff, you know? Gotta say, it's fun.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T13:26:38-08:00","id":1188280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wormwood</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1186420-favourite-satiety-conditions?p=1188254#post1188254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Only way I see resolving Slumbering easily is with Looming Presence or Relentless Hunter: upon being full with Lethal, the Horror awakens, upon which either there are now unchecked health boxes (+Lair due to Looming Presence being in effect once more) or you can spend 1 Satiety to erease all Lethal and Bashing damage, making this a fairly easy method to return from Slumbering.</div> </div> </div> </div>Full agreement here. Of course, even better if you have both Atavisms. As a matter of fact, this is the only strategy to resolve the Slumbering condition that I do not deem borderline suicidal, or so costly the benefits of Slumbering are absolutely not worth it. Other reasons I avoid Slumbering most of the time is using it too much negates the whole point of playing a Beast and I find the requirements of high-Satiety feeding too burdensome in most circumstances. But I'm not going to deny that once in a great while, 'going mortal' might be useful and valuable for some of the reasons you previously argued about.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-24T04:45:45-08:00","id":1189194,"title":"","content":"As far as resolving Slumbering goes I noticed there's a rather significant question left unanswered by the book: what if someone <i>else</i> roughs up your Horror for you?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-24T05:25:50-08:00","id":1189200,"title":"","content":"Same thing, I would imagine. Question is, why would anyone try that? Maybe you called in a favour. Maybe it is just a lucky coincidence saving you some trouble.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-24T05:38:37-08:00","id":1189203,"title":"","content":"Or maybe in you're, you know, in a brood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-24T06:00:12-08:00","id":1189206,"title":"","content":"Well, yes. It's not like I, for a brief moment, completely forgot about the fact that Broodmates share access to their Lairs and stuff... &gt;.&lt;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}